This confirms the exact bug pattern: `taskExecutionStats` correctly computes elapsed time via `MathUtils.elapsedMicroSec(startNanos)` before registering, but `taskPendingStats.registerSuccessfulEvent(initNanos, ...)` directly passes the raw timestamp `initNanos` instead of the elapsed duration since `initNanos`. This is the exact same bug fixed in the 2c001a55 commit, in the same code (just moved/renamed into `OrderedScheduler.java` later).

## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: 21ceb4c958fd3ea71de6229e878c5560c9f94385

## Reasoning
(1) **Investigation**: The fix in HEAD (2c001a55) changes `taskPendingStats.registerSuccessfulEvent(initNanos, TimeUnit.NANOSECONDS);` to `taskPendingStats.registerSuccessfulEvent(MathUtils.elapsedNanos(initNanos), TimeUnit.NANOSECONDS);` in `OrderedScheduler.java`. This is clearly a bug fix for a "pass absolute timestamp instead of elapsed duration" mistake. I used `git log -p --follow` on `OrderedScheduler.java` to trace the history of this exact line back through file renames (it used to be `OrderedSafeExecutor.java`), and found the line's origin.

(2) **Specific change**: In commit `21ceb4c958fd3ea71de6229e878c5560c9f94385` (Oct 6, 2015, "add stats tracking to OrderedSafeExecutor" style refactor), the `TimedRunnable.safeRun()` method was introduced with:
```java
taskPendingStats.registerSuccessfulEvent(initNanos, TimeUnit.NANOSECONDS);
long startNanos = MathUtils.nowInNano();
this.runnable.safeRun();
long elapsedMicroSec = MathUtils.elapsedMicroSec(startNanos);
taskExecutionStats.registerSuccessfulEvent(elapsedMicroSec, TimeUnit.MICROSECONDS);
```
The author correctly computed `elapsedMicroSec` via `MathUtils.elapsedMicroSec(startNanos)` for `taskExecutionStats`, but for `taskPendingStats` (queue wait time), they passed the raw `initNanos` timestamp directly rather than first computing the elapsed time since task creation (e.g. via `MathUtils.elapsedNanos(initNanos)`). This means "pending time" stats recorded an ever-growing absolute nanosecond timestamp instead of the actual queue-wait duration — a copy/paste-style bug.

(3) **Supporting evidence**: `git show 21ceb4c958fd3ea71de6229e878c5560c9f94385 -- .../OrderedSafeExecutor.java` shows this exact faulty line being added for the first time, alongside the correctly-computed sibling statistic (`taskExecutionStats`), demonstrating the inconsistency was present from the moment this code was authored. The line persisted unchanged (just moved to `OrderedScheduler.java` during later refactors) until it was fixed in 2c001a55.

I'm highly confident this is the introducing commit since it's the literal first appearance of the exact buggy expression, with no intermediate modification to that line in between.

